import std

| Tutorial section 5 brain: harvester + upgrader + builder + tower, plus
| the generic-interpreter emit contract. The shell is a dumb fold: it
| reads this file's exports (spawnQueue, behaviors, the four machines,
| extension + tower placement policy) and executes — it decides NOTHING.
| The tower is the first NON-CREEP machine: same Machine/StateConfig/
| Transition shape, its own event vocabulary, driven by the shell in a
| separate structure loop.

union CreepRole
  harvester
  upgrader
  builder

union BodyPart
  work
  carry
  move

| State namespace design. Each machine is verified over its OWN state
| union (generate rejects a machine whose state type has states the
| machine never reaches), so machine states are per-machine unions:
|
|   HarvesterState: harvesting, delivering, supporting
|   UpgraderState:  collecting, upgrading
|   BuilderState:   gathering, building, assisting
|   TowerState:     guarding, attacking, repairing
|
| CreepState is the shared NAMESPACE union over the eight CREEP names.
| It is used only by the flat creep semantics — StateBehaviors totality
| and RoleSpec.initial — never as a machine state type. TowerState is
| deliberately NOT part of CreepState: the tower is not a creep, has its
| own behaviors record (TowerStateBehaviors), and the shell drives
| towers in a separate loop. In generated TS all of these are
| string-literal types, so each role union is assignable to CreepState.
|
| Disjointness across ALL machines — creep roles AND the tower — is
| load-bearing: the shell's machine registry uses the generated
| validHarvesterState / validUpgraderState / validBuilderState /
| validTowerState functions as OWNERSHIP GUARDS — an actor whose state
| fails a machine's valid*State check is foreign to that machine and the
| machine is skipped. If two machines ever shared a state name,
| ownership would be ambiguous — don't merge variants. That is why the
| harvester's full-and-nowhere-to-deliver state is `supporting`, not
| `assisting`: the builder already owns `assisting`.

union HarvesterState
  harvesting
  delivering
  supporting

union UpgraderState
  collecting
  upgrading

union BuilderState
  gathering
  building
  assisting

union TowerState
  guarding
  attacking
  repairing

union CreepState
  harvesting
  delivering
  supporting
  collecting
  upgrading
  gathering
  building
  assisting

| The shell observes the world and emits exactly ONE CreepEvent per
| creep per tick. There is NO priority and NO masking: the event is the
| PRODUCT of three orthogonal facts, all of which ride every event.
|
|   store level  — empty     creep has zero energy
|                  mid       neither empty nor full
|                  full      creep has zero free capacity
|   sink status  — SinksOpen some energy sink passes the free-energy
|                            filter
|                  SinksFull NO energy sink in the room passes the
|                            free-energy filter
|   site status  — Site      construction sites exist
|                  NoSite    no construction sites exist
|
| "Energy sink" = spawn + extensions + towers BELOW their energy
| threshold (see towerContext: a tower is a sink only while its energy
| < energyTarget — the same one filter drives delivery targeting AND
| this observation, so the fact can never contradict the action).
|
| NAMING CONTRACT (exact casing is load-bearing): each variant is the
| literal concatenation storeLevel + sinkStatus + siteStatus. The shell
| computes the event as the TypeScript template literal
|
|   `${store}${sinks}${sites}`
|
| with store in {"empty","mid","full"}, sinks in
| {"SinksOpen","SinksFull"}, sites in {"Site","NoSite"} — and tsc proves
| the inferred template-literal union is EXACTLY the generated
| CreepEvent union. Rename a variant here and the shell fails to
| typecheck; add a fourth store level and every machine below fails
| generate's coverage check. Two checkers, one vocabulary.
|
| WHY a product instead of the old priority chain
| (storeEmpty > sinksFull > storeFull > sawSite|noSites): priority
| masking caused two live bugs.
|
|   1. The FROZEN builder: storeFull outranked the residual noSites, so
|      a full builder in `building` with zero sites kept receiving
|      storeFull, self-looped, resolved no build target, and idled
|      until TTL death.
|   2. The BLIND builder: sinksFull outranked sawSite, so a full
|      builder under sink saturation could never observe that sites
|      existed — the one fact its role runs on.
|
| With a product, masking is UNREPRESENTABLE: every event carries the
| store level AND the sink status AND the site status. There is nothing
| left to outrank. The old compound sinksFull ("store full AND sinks
| full") dissolves into the fullSinksFull* corner of the product, and
| the old residual pair sawSite/noSites becomes the always-present
| Site/NoSite dimension. The observe-before-act property that retired
| the tutorial-2 ERR_FULL recovery is unchanged: the shell still reads
| sink fullness before any transfer is attempted.
|
| Variant order is systematic: store level outermost (empty, mid,
| full), then sinks (Open, Full), then sites (Site, NoSite).

union CreepEvent
  emptySinksOpenSite
  emptySinksOpenNoSite
  emptySinksFullSite
  emptySinksFullNoSite
  midSinksOpenSite
  midSinksOpenNoSite
  midSinksFullSite
  midSinksFullNoSite
  fullSinksOpenSite
  fullSinksOpenNoSite
  fullSinksFullSite
  fullSinksFullNoSite

| The FIXED action vocabulary the shell knows how to execute. States
| are tagged with actions (see StateBehaviors / TowerStateBehaviors); a
| novel state added by a future spec mutation still maps into this
| vocabulary, so the shell never needs new wiring. idle pairs with
| TargetKind.none and is the tower's guarding behavior. attack and
| repair are tower verbs — creep states never use them today, but the
| vocabulary is shared: one Action union, one shell switch.

union Action
  harvest
  transfer
  upgrade
  build
  attack
  repair
  idle

| The FIXED target-selector vocabulary. The shell resolves each kind:
|   source           — closest active energy source
|   energySink       — closest spawn|extension|below-threshold tower
|                      with free energy capacity (towers count only
|                      while energy < towerContext.energyTarget)
|   controller       — the room controller
|   constructionSite — first construction site
|   hostile          — closest hostile creep from the actor's pos
|   damagedStructure — closest owned-room structure with hits < hitsMax
|   none             — no target (pairs with Action.idle)

union TargetKind
  source
  energySink
  controller
  constructionSite
  hostile
  damagedStructure
  none

| A state's behavior: what to do and at what.

type Behavior
  action: Action
  target: TargetKind

| StateBehaviors is a RECORD with one field per CreepState variant —
| deliberately NOT a map. When the shell indexes behaviors[state], tsc
| proves totality: add a state without a behavior and the build fails.

type StateBehaviors
  harvesting: Behavior
  delivering: Behavior
  supporting: Behavior
  collecting: Behavior
  upgrading: Behavior
  gathering: Behavior
  building: Behavior
  assisting: Behavior

harvestingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.harvest
    target: TargetKind.source

deliveringBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.transfer
    target: TargetKind.energySink

| supporting = the harvester's full-and-nowhere-to-deliver escape:
| spend the load on the controller instead of stalling on an empty
| transfer-target set. Same action pair as the builder's assisting, but
| its own name — cross-machine state disjointness is load-bearing (see
| the namespace comment above).

supportingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.upgrade
    target: TargetKind.controller

collectingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.harvest
    target: TargetKind.source

upgradingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.upgrade
    target: TargetKind.controller

gatheringBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.harvest
    target: TargetKind.source

buildingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.build
    target: TargetKind.constructionSite

assistingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.upgrade
    target: TargetKind.controller

behaviors: StateBehaviors
  StateBehaviors:
    harvesting: harvestingBehavior
    delivering: deliveringBehavior
    supporting: supportingBehavior
    collecting: collectingBehavior
    upgrading: upgradingBehavior
    gathering: gatheringBehavior
    building: buildingBehavior
    assisting: assistingBehavior

| Population policy. spawnQueue's ORDER is the spawn priority: the
| shell walks it front to back and spawns the first role below its
| desired count. No if/else chain in the shell — this list IS the
| policy.
|
| Capacity-tiered bodies. Part costs: work=100, carry=50, move=50.
|
|   heavyWorkerBody = [work, work, carry, move, move]  cost 350
|   workerBody      = [work, carry, move]              cost 200
|
| 350 is exactly the current room energyCapacityAvailable (spawn 300 +
| 1 extension 50). A tier list is ORDERED, richest first — the order IS
| the policy: the shell walks bodies front to back and spawns the FIRST
| tier whose cost fits the afford basis (see spawnAffordBasis below).
| Mechanical maximization over spec data, same pattern as
| extensionOffsets: the spec ranks, the world disposes.

workerBody: [BodyPart]
  [BodyPart.work, BodyPart.carry, BodyPart.move]

heavyWorkerBody: [BodyPart]
  [BodyPart.work, BodyPart.work, BodyPart.carry, BodyPart.move, BodyPart.move]

workerBodyTiers: [[BodyPart]]
  [heavyWorkerBody, workerBody]

| Afford basis: which Room energy figure a spawn attempt is priced
| against. The variant names are EXACT Screeps Room property names —
| the generated string literal indexes directly into the Room type
| (room[spawnAffordBasis]), no shell switch.
|
| Decision: afford against CURRENT energy (energyAvailable), not
| capacity. Waiting for capacity deadlocks recovery: with all
| harvesters dead, the extension never refills, so 350 never arrives
| and nothing ever spawns. At steady state the sinks are full, so the
| rich tier still spawns — capacity gets spent without risking
| starvation.

union SpawnAffordBasis
  energyAvailable
  energyCapacityAvailable

spawnAffordBasis: SpawnAffordBasis
  SpawnAffordBasis.energyAvailable

type RoleSpec
  role: CreepRole
  desired: Integer
  bodies: [[BodyPart]]
  initial: CreepState

harvesterSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.harvester
    desired: 2
    bodies: workerBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.harvesting

upgraderSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.upgrader
    desired: 1
    bodies: workerBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.collecting

builderSpec: RoleSpec
  RoleSpec:
    role: CreepRole.builder
    desired: 2
    bodies: workerBodyTiers
    initial: CreepState.gathering

spawnQueue: [RoleSpec]
  [harvesterSpec, upgraderSpec, builderSpec]

| Extension placement policy. The shell places construction sites for
| extensions until desiredExtensions exist (built or in progress),
| trying extensionOffsets in order relative to Spawn1 until one sticks
| — terrain may reject a candidate, so the list is ordered fallbacks.

desiredExtensions: Integer
  1

type SiteOffset
  dx: Integer
  dy: Integer

offsetEast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 2
    dy: 0

offsetWest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -2
    dy: 0

offsetSouth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: 2

offsetNorth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: -2

offsetSoutheast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 2
    dy: 2

offsetNorthwest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -2
    dy: -2

extensionOffsets: [SiteOffset]
  [offsetEast, offsetWest, offsetSouth, offsetNorth, offsetSoutheast, offsetNorthwest]

| Tower placement policy — brain data, mirrors the extension mechanism
| exactly: the shell places STRUCTURE_TOWER construction sites at these
| offsets from Spawn1 until desiredTowers exist (built or in progress),
| once RCL allows it (towers unlock at RCL 3); server legality decides
| whether a candidate sticks, so the list is ordered fallbacks. The
| offsets are chosen to be DISJOINT from every extensionOffsets cell —
| extensions own (2,0),(-2,0),(0,2),(0,-2),(2,2),(-2,-2); towers take
| the remaining diagonals and the distance-3 cardinals.

desiredTowers: Integer
  1

offsetSouthwest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -2
    dy: 2

offsetNortheast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 2
    dy: -2

offsetFarSouth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: 3

offsetFarEast: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 3
    dy: 0

offsetFarNorth: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: 0
    dy: -3

offsetFarWest: SiteOffset
  SiteOffset:
    dx: -3
    dy: 0

towerOffsets: [SiteOffset]
  [offsetSouthwest, offsetNortheast, offsetFarSouth, offsetFarEast, offsetFarNorth, offsetFarWest]

| Per-creep FSMs. Three creep machines share CreepEvent/CreepContext
| but each has its OWN state union, so generate verifies each machine
| total and reachable over exactly its own states. Every state handles
| every one of the 12 events, self-loops written out — total and
| explicit, no implicit fallthrough. Transitions are named top-level
| values and REUSED across states of the same machine wherever
| event+target coincide: state names are disjoint across all machines,
| so a name like toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite is globally unambiguous —
| the target names the machine.

| Context carries the same tier list as RoleSpec.bodies — a machine's
| context must never contradict the spawn policy, so there is no
| single-body field anywhere.

type CreepContext
  role: CreepRole
  bodies: [[BodyPart]]

harvesterContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.harvester
  bodies: workerBodyTiers

upgraderContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.upgrader
  bodies: workerBodyTiers

builderContext: CreepContext
  role: CreepRole.builder
  bodies: workerBodyTiers

| Harvester machine: harvest until full, deliver until empty; nowhere
| to put it -> support the controller.
|
| The supporting state is the full-and-nowhere-to-deliver escape. Two
| routes in:
|
|   - full + SinksFull anywhere: a full harvester with a saturated sink
|     set has an EMPTY transfer-target set; delivering would be a
|     null-target stall.
|   - mid + SinksFull while delivering or supporting (CRITICAL): a
|     mid-store harvester in delivering whose sinks just saturated is
|     the exact freeze signature of the old builder bug — the behavior
|     says transfer, the target set is empty, no API call happens, the
|     store never drains, the event never changes. Staying put is a
|     null-target stall; spend the remainder on the controller instead.
|
| mid + SinksOpen while supporting goes BACK to delivering: sinks
| reopened, and delivery outranks controller-feeding for this role —
| it powers spawning. Site presence is noise to this role: every
| transition below is Site/NoSite-symmetric.

| — empty in any harvester state: back to the source (8 values; the
|   mid* four are the harvesting self-loops).

toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

toHarvestingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.harvesting
    guard: ()

| — open sinks with energy on board: deliver (mid* used by delivering/
|   supporting, full* used by all three states).

toDeliveringMidSinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

toDeliveringMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.delivering
    guard: ()

| — saturated sinks with energy on board: support the controller (the
|   null-target-stall escapes documented above).

toSupportingMidSinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

toSupportingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

toSupportingFullSinksFullSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite: Transition HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullNoSite
    target: HarvesterState.supporting
    guard: ()

harvestingConfig: StateConfig HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toHarvestingMidSinksOpenSite, toHarvestingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingMidSinksFullSite, toHarvestingMidSinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Harvesting"

| delivering and supporting have IDENTICAL transition tables (empty ->
| harvesting; SinksOpen with energy -> delivering; SinksFull with
| energy -> supporting): both states are "energy on board, dispose of
| it", and where to dispose depends only on the sink dimension.

deliveringConfig: StateConfig HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Delivering"

supportingConfig: StateConfig HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullSite, toHarvestingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringMidSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullSite, toSupportingMidSinksFullNoSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenSite, toDeliveringFullSinksOpenNoSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullSite, toSupportingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Supporting"

harvesterMachine: Machine HarvesterState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "harvester"
    initial: HarvesterState.harvesting
    context: harvesterContext
    finals: {}
    states: {HarvesterState.harvesting: harvestingConfig, HarvesterState.delivering: deliveringConfig, HarvesterState.supporting: supportingConfig}

| Upgrader machine: collect until full, upgrade until empty. The sink
| and site dimensions are IRRELEVANT to this role — it delivers to the
| controller, not the sinks, and it never builds — so every transition
| depends only on the store level: full anywhere -> upgrading, empty
| anywhere -> collecting, mid self-loops.
|
| The old collectingSinksFull -> upgrading shortcut dissolves here: the
| old compound sinksFull only fired when the creep's store was ALSO
| full, so it was never anything but a proxy for "store full" wearing a
| sink costume. Under the product vocabulary the proxy is exposed —
| full* covers every case the shortcut ever handled, and the sink
| dimension goes back to being noise for this role.

| — full in any upgrader state: go upgrade (also the upgrading full
|   self-loops).

toUpgradingFullSinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingFullSinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingFullSinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingFullSinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

| — empty in any upgrader state: back to the source (also the
|   collecting empty self-loops).

toCollectingEmptySinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingEmptySinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingEmptySinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingEmptySinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

| — mid self-loops: keep doing what you're doing.

toCollectingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingMidSinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toCollectingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.collecting
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksFullSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

toUpgradingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: UpgraderState.upgrading
    guard: ()

collectingConfig: StateConfig UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toCollectingEmptySinksOpenSite, toCollectingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toCollectingMidSinksOpenSite, toCollectingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toCollectingMidSinksFullSite, toCollectingMidSinksFullNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Collecting"

upgradingConfig: StateConfig UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toCollectingEmptySinksOpenSite, toCollectingEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullSite, toCollectingEmptySinksFullNoSite, toUpgradingMidSinksOpenSite, toUpgradingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toUpgradingMidSinksFullSite, toUpgradingMidSinksFullNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenSite, toUpgradingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullSite, toUpgradingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Upgrading"

upgraderMachine: Machine UpgraderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "upgrader"
    initial: UpgraderState.collecting
    context: upgraderContext
    finals: {}
    states: {UpgraderState.collecting: collectingConfig, UpgraderState.upgrading: upgradingConfig}

| Builder machine: gather until full; full -> build if sites exist,
| else assist; never freeze, never go blind. The sink dimension is
| irrelevant to this role — a builder consumes energy on sites and the
| controller, it never transfers — so every transition below is
| SinksOpen/SinksFull-symmetric.
|
| BOTH historical bugs of the priority-chain vocabulary are now
| UNREPRESENTABLE, and this machine is where they lived:
|
|   1. The FROZEN builder (historical): under the old chain, storeFull
|      outranked the residual noSites, so a full builder in building
|      with zero sites kept receiving storeFull. The old building
|      self-loop on storeFull was an unreachable-escape freeze: full
|      builder, zero sites, action=build resolving no target, idle
|      until TTL death (births 105 / deaths 100 on the live server).
|      The eventual fix routed building --storeFull--> assisting — an
|      EXACT move only because of the timing argument: events are
|      computed at tick start, before actions, and an honest builder
|      entering building spends energy the same tick, so full while IN
|      building implies the previous build spent nothing. Under the
|      product that inference is unnecessary: full*NoSite says
|      directly "full and no site exists" — the escape is observed,
|      not deduced.
|   2. The BLIND builder (historical): the old compound sinksFull
|      outranked sawSite, so a full builder under sink saturation
|      could never see a site. Now fullSinksFullSite -> building: the
|      site fact rides the event no matter what the sinks are doing.
|
| full*Site while ALREADY IN building stays in building: by the same
| timing argument, an honest builder should not observe full here — if
| it does, the previous build spent nothing (site unreachable this
| tick, ramp-up ordering, etc.). But the site is VISIBLE, and staying
| on it is honest retry against an observed target — not masking. The
| freeze case was full+NoSite being masked, and that now has its own
| pair of events routing to assisting.
|
| mid*NoSite while building -> assisting: the last site finished
| mid-drain; spend the remainder on the controller instead of
| null-target stalling. Spec decision unchanged from section 3: no
| wasted creep-ticks. mid*Site while assisting pulls the builder back
| to building.

| — empty in any builder state: back to the source (the mid* four are
|   the gathering self-loops).

toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.emptySinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

toGatheringMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.gathering
    guard: ()

| — a site exists and there is energy on board: build (mid* used by
|   building/assisting, full* used by all three states — including the
|   formerly-blind fullSinksFullSite).

toBuildingMidSinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

toBuildingMidSinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

toBuildingFullSinksFullSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullSite
    target: BuilderState.building
    guard: ()

| — no site and energy on board: assist the controller (the formerly-
|   frozen full+NoSite corner, now directly observed).

toAssistingMidSinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

toAssistingMidSinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.midSinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksOpenNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite: Transition BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Transition:
    event: CreepEvent.fullSinksFullNoSite
    target: BuilderState.assisting
    guard: ()

gatheringConfig: StateConfig BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite, toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite, toGatheringMidSinksOpenSite, toGatheringMidSinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringMidSinksFullSite, toGatheringMidSinksFullNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite, toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksFullSite, toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Gathering"

| building and assisting have IDENTICAL transition tables (empty ->
| gathering; energy + Site -> building; energy + NoSite -> assisting):
| both states are "energy on board, spend it", and where to spend
| depends only on the site dimension.

buildingConfig: StateConfig BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite, toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksOpenSite, toAssistingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksFullSite, toAssistingMidSinksFullNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite, toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksFullSite, toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Building"

assistingConfig: StateConfig BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toGatheringEmptySinksOpenSite, toGatheringEmptySinksOpenNoSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullSite, toGatheringEmptySinksFullNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksOpenSite, toAssistingMidSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingMidSinksFullSite, toAssistingMidSinksFullNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksOpenSite, toAssistingFullSinksOpenNoSite, toBuildingFullSinksFullSite, toAssistingFullSinksFullNoSite]
    label: "Assisting"

builderMachine: Machine BuilderState CreepEvent CreepContext
  Machine:
    name: "builder"
    initial: BuilderState.gathering
    context: builderContext
    finals: {}
    states: {BuilderState.gathering: gatheringConfig, BuilderState.building: buildingConfig, BuilderState.assisting: assistingConfig}

| ============================== TOWER ===============================
| The first non-creep machine. Same Machine/StateConfig/Transition
| shape as the creep machines, its own event and context types; the
| shell drives towers in a SEPARATE loop (one TowerEvent per tower per
| tick), with validTowerState as the ownership guard, exactly like the
| creep registry.

| TowerEvent is a product of two orthogonal facts, same construction
| and same template-literal contract as CreepEvent:
|
|   threat    — hostile  hostile creeps present in the room
|               calm     none
|   integrity — Damage   some owned-room structure has hits < hitsMax
|               Intact   none
|
| The shell computes the event as `${threat}${integrity}` with threat
| in {"hostile","calm"} and integrity in {"Damage","Intact"}, and tsc
| proves the inferred union is exactly the generated TowerEvent union.
|
| DELIBERATE EXCLUSION: tower energy is NOT an event dimension. No
| tower decision depends on it — a drained tower's attack or repair is
| a harmless no-op (the intent fails, nothing worse), and recovery is
| delivery-driven: the tower re-enters the energy-sink set the moment
| its energy drops below energyTarget, and harvesters refill it through
| the ordinary delivery path. Including an energy dimension would
| double the table for zero behavioral difference.

union TowerEvent
  hostileDamage
  hostileIntact
  calmDamage
  calmIntact

| The threshold-sink decision. A tower is an energy SINK only while its
| energy < energyTarget. The shell uses this ONE filter for BOTH
| delivery targeting (TargetKind.energySink resolution) and the
| SinksFull observation, so the observed fact can never contradict the
| available action. Why a threshold and not full capacity (1000): the
| 500..1000 band is attack/repair buffer that nobody eagerly refills —
| with full-capacity sink semantics, every point of repair drain would
| reopen the sink set and flap saturation forever (a leaky sink that
| keeps the harvesters chasing it). Below 500 the tower is a real
| delivery target; above it, it's on its own buffer.
|
| TowerContext carries desired alongside energyTarget: Paradox rejects
| single-field product types (error 23; the alternative, wrap, would
| emit a branded scalar and change the shell-facing shape). desired
| REFERENCES desiredTowers — the context must never contradict the
| placement policy, same pattern as CreepContext.bodies referencing
| workerBodyTiers.

type TowerContext
  energyTarget: Integer
  desired: Integer

towerEnergyTarget: Integer
  500

towerContext: TowerContext
  energyTarget: towerEnergyTarget
  desired: desiredTowers

| Tower policy: attack OUTRANKS repair. The reference implementation
| (tutorial section 5 main.js) issues repair and then attack in the
| same tick, where the later attack intent wins anyway — engine
| accident as priority. We make the priority explicit and verified:
| hostile present -> attacking, no matter the integrity fact.
|
| The mapping is STATE-INDEPENDENT — all three states carry the
| IDENTICAL four transitions, so this is a pure policy function
| (TowerEvent -> TowerState) in FSM clothing. That is deliberate: the
| uniform machine shape keeps the shell a generic interpreter — one
| registry, one transition call, no special-casing the tower. The
| current state still matters downstream: towerBehaviors[state] picks
| the action each tick.

toAttackingHostileDamage: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.hostileDamage
    target: TowerState.attacking
    guard: ()

toAttackingHostileIntact: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.hostileIntact
    target: TowerState.attacking
    guard: ()

toRepairingCalmDamage: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.calmDamage
    target: TowerState.repairing
    guard: ()

toGuardingCalmIntact: Transition TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Transition:
    event: TowerEvent.calmIntact
    target: TowerState.guarding
    guard: ()

guardingConfig: StateConfig TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toAttackingHostileDamage, toAttackingHostileIntact, toRepairingCalmDamage, toGuardingCalmIntact]
    label: "Guarding"

attackingConfig: StateConfig TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toAttackingHostileDamage, toAttackingHostileIntact, toRepairingCalmDamage, toGuardingCalmIntact]
    label: "Attacking"

repairingConfig: StateConfig TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  StateConfig:
    transitions: [toAttackingHostileDamage, toAttackingHostileIntact, toRepairingCalmDamage, toGuardingCalmIntact]
    label: "Repairing"

| Reachability from guarding: hostile* -> attacking, calmDamage ->
| repairing — all three states reachable, every state covers all four
| events, no finals, no deadlock. generate verifies exactly this.

towerMachine: Machine TowerState TowerEvent TowerContext
  Machine:
    name: "tower"
    initial: TowerState.guarding
    context: towerContext
    finals: {}
    states: {TowerState.guarding: guardingConfig, TowerState.attacking: attackingConfig, TowerState.repairing: repairingConfig}

| TowerStateBehaviors is a RECORD with one field per TowerState variant
| — the same tsc-totality trick as StateBehaviors: the shell indexes
| towerBehaviors[state], and adding a tower state without a behavior
| fails the build.

type TowerStateBehaviors
  guarding: Behavior
  attacking: Behavior
  repairing: Behavior

guardingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.idle
    target: TargetKind.none

attackingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.attack
    target: TargetKind.hostile

repairingBehavior: Behavior
  Behavior:
    action: Action.repair
    target: TargetKind.damagedStructure

towerBehaviors: TowerStateBehaviors
  TowerStateBehaviors:
    guarding: guardingBehavior
    attacking: attackingBehavior
    repairing: repairingBehavior
